{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794520261,"nonce":"iO+Dpu2DQ3vNboBeaxoAhgjWjow=","title":"iOS: build and run an ash program with only Xcode and the iOS SDK installed","message":"Requirement (owner, 2026-11-12): iOS-specific support and tooling, so a developer needs little setup beyond the iOS SDK (Xcode).\n\nChecked 2026-11-12 on a Mac with Xcode 26.4, the iPhoneOS SDK and the Rust iOS targets installed:\n- The compiler emits Mach-O arm64 objects for aarch64-apple-ios and aarch64-apple-ios-sim from test_basic.hl. The two objects are byte-identical and neither carries an LC_BUILD_VERSION or LC_VERSION_MIN command, so device versus simulator is not expressed in the output; target_abi.rs does not mention iOS at all.\n- The runtime does not build as shipped: `cargo check -p ash_std --target aarch64-apple-ios` stops in std/build.rs (header discovery) wanting `aarch64-apple-ios-gcc`. With `BINDGEN_EXTRA_CLANG_ARGS_aarch64_apple_ios=\"--target=arm64-apple-ios --sysroot=$(xcrun --sdk iphoneos --show-sdk-path)\"` the check finishes cleanly (type-check only).\n\nNot checked: linking, signing, running on a simulator or device, the simulator on an Intel Mac (x86_64-apple-ios), the fiber switch, setjmp traps and unwinding on iOS, how natives are bound.\n\nConstraints to verify, from general platform knowledge and not from this repo: an iOS app does not generate code at run time under the normal policy, which would leave the interpreter and ahead-of-time code and rule out the JIT tiers; a dynamically loaded .hdll is not available the way it is on desktop, so natives would have to be linked in statically, as Caribou's AOT link does.\n\nMissing today: release.yml builds no runtime for an iOS triple; the native cross link in aot_link.rs has no Apple mobile case; there is no packaging step.\n\nAcceptance, from the requirement:\n1. Find the SDK without configuration: `xcrun --sdk iphoneos|iphonesimulator --show-sdk-path` and `xcode-select`.\n2. std/build.rs takes the target's headers from that SDK with no gcc and no env var.\n3. Device and simulator are distinct outputs (the platform tag in the object, `-ios-simulator` in the triple), and runtimes exist for device arm64, simulator arm64 and simulator x86_64.\n4. `ash --build --target \u003cios triple\u003e` links with `xcrun clang` and writes a static library or an XCFramework a developer adds to an Xcode project.\n5. An entry shim a Swift or Objective-C app calls, and a decision on what Xcode does the rest of (signing stays with Xcode). Open question for the owner.\n6. `ash run` on a simulator through `xcrun simctl`, and a CI job on a macOS runner that does it.\n\nShared with the Android issue: a C entry point for starting the runtime from a host app, and the per-target runtimes in the release workflow.","files":null}]}